THIS IS THE CAUSE OF THE BOOT HANG (~1 out of 4).

Found on 2026-08-15 with the full dump pstore gave after a watchdog reset. The
trace, at 1.75 s of kernel:

    Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address ffff8000811ff000
    Internal error: Oops: 0000000096000007 [#1] PREEMPT SMP
    CPU: 4 PID: 138 Comm: kworker/4:5   Tainted: G  W
    Workqueue: events nt36xxx_download_firmware
    pc : nt36xxx_download_firmware+0x6cc/0xb10
    Call trace:
     nt36xxx_download_firmware+0x6cc/0xb10
     process_scheduled_works+0x1c0/0x494
     worker_thread+0x28c/0x420

It has nothing to do with audio, nor with the ADSP, nor with the LPI, which is
where we had been looking since 2026-08-10. It is the touchscreen.

THE FAULT
---------
In _nt36xxx_boot_download_firmware():

    ts->fw_entry.data = devm_kmemdup(dev, fw_entry->data, fw_entry->size, ...);

    release_firmware(fw_entry);          <- frees fw_entry AND fw_entry->data

    if (!ts->fw_entry.data) { ... }
    ts->fw_entry.size = fw_entry->size;                    <- USE AFTER FREE
    WARN_ON(ts->fw_entry.data[0] != fw_entry->data[0]);    <- USE AFTER FREE

It releases the firmware and afterwards reads its size and dereferences its
pointer.

WHY IT IS INTERMITTENT, AND WHY IT WAS SO HARD TO SEE
-----------------------------------------------------
Almost always the just-freed memory keeps the old values, so `size` comes out
right and the boot goes through. But when something reuses that memory first
-and at boot there is a huge amount of allocation activity-, `ts->fw_entry.size`
comes out garbage. And then the loop below:

    for (i = (ts->fw_entry.size / 4096); i > 0; i--)
            strncmp(&ts->fw_entry.data[i * 4096 - 3], "NVT", 3)

runs far past the end of the buffer until it leaves what is mapped: a paging
fault at ffff8000811ff000, which is the exact address of the Oops.

It happens at **1.75 s**, before the initramfs brings up the USB gadget
(2.45 s). That is why the phone "does not re-enumerate": it dies before it has
network, before journald and before the modules are loaded. It left no trace
anywhere.

THE FIX
-------
Save the size before releasing the firmware, and remove the WARN_ON, which
dereferences freed memory just to compare one byte. That WARN_ON is also the
"Tainted: [W]" that shows up in the trace.

HOW IT IS CHECKED
-----------------
surya/boot/reboot-loop 30. The failure rate before the patch was between
25% and 40% depending on the run.

--- a/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36xxx_core.c
+++ b/drivers/input/touchscreen/nt36xxx_core.c
@@ -709,6 +709,7 @@
 static void _nt36xxx_boot_download_firmware(struct nt36xxx_ts *ts) {
 	int i, ret, retry = 0;
 	size_t fw_need_write_size = 0;
+	size_t fw_size;
 	const struct firmware *fw_entry;
 	u8 val[8 * 4] = {0};

@@ -730,7 +731,13 @@
 	 * pm_resume need to re-upload fw for NT36675 IC
 	 *
 	 */
-	ts->fw_entry.data = devm_kmemdup(ts->dev, fw_entry->data, fw_entry->size, GFP_KERNEL | GFP_DMA);
+	/*
+	 * Keep the size BEFORE releasing the firmware: release_firmware()
+	 * frees fw_entry and its data, and the code below kept reading them.
+	 */
+	fw_size = fw_entry->size;
+
+	ts->fw_entry.data = devm_kmemdup(ts->dev, fw_entry->data, fw_size, GFP_KERNEL | GFP_DMA);

 	release_firmware(fw_entry);

@@ -738,9 +745,7 @@
 		dev_err(ts->dev, "memdup fw_data fail\n");
 		goto exit;
 	}
-	ts->fw_entry.size = fw_entry->size;
-
-	WARN_ON(ts->fw_entry.data[0] != fw_entry->data[0]);
+	ts->fw_entry.size = fw_size;

 	for (i = (ts->fw_entry.size / 4096); i > 0; i--) {
 		if (strncmp(&ts->fw_entry.data[i * 4096 - 3], "NVT", 3) == 0) {
